<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p>Is each set empty, finite (but not empty) or infinite?</p><p>(a) <m>{\{x : x \text{ is a natural number}, x + 7 = 7\}}</m> [ empty | finite, not empty | infinite ]  </p><p>(b) <m>{\{x : x \text{ is a whole number}, x + 7 = 7\}}</m> [ empty | finite, not empty | infinite ]  </p><p>(c) <m>{\{x : x \text{ is a multiple of 7}\}}</m> [ empty | finite, not empty | infinite ]  </p></p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>First ask whether the set has any elements at all. If it has, ask whether the counting would ever stop.</p><p>(a) <m>x + 7 = 7</m> gives <m>x = 0</m>, which is not a natural number, so the set is empty.</p><p>(b) <m>x = 0</m> is a whole number, so the set is <m>\{0\}</m>: finite, not empty.</p><p>(c) <m>7, 14, 21, \ldots</m> go on for ever: infinite.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p>empty</p></answer>
    <answer><p>finite, not empty</p></answer>
    <answer><p>infinite</p></answer>
</stack-static>